WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.509
Picture the front desk of a small town library.

2
00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:11.486
To borrow books, you fill in a loan slip: your member number, the titles, and when they're due back.

3
00:00:11.486 --> 00:00:14.834
It sits in a clear sleeve, so anyone can read every box.

4
00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:18.241
Two slips filled in the same way stand for the same loan.

5
00:00:18.241 --> 00:00:21.229
And the clerk checks each slip before stamping it.

6
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.215
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:25.215 --> 00:00:27.293
People say records save typing.

8
00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:30.174
But I can write equals and hashCode myself.

9
00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:32.251
So is a record just a shortcut?

10
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:39.418
Let's start small, with a shelfmark: the label on a book's spine that says where the book lives.

11
00:00:39.418 --> 00:00:42.904
A three-letter section and a shelf number, like FIC 12.

12
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:46.122
Here's the class written the old way, by hand.

13
00:00:46.122 --> 00:00:52.163
It lists its data in five places: the fields, the constructor, equals, hashCode and toString.

14
00:00:52.163 --> 00:00:55.996
At first there was only a section, so all five say section.

15
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.417
Then the shelf number was added.

16
00:00:58.417 --> 00:01:01.465
Someone added the field and the constructor.

17
00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:03.820
And equals, hashCode and toString?

18
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.205
Nobody updated them.

19
00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:06.452
It still compiles.

20
00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:10.972
So take two books, one on FIC 12 and one on FIC 40.

21
00:01:10.972 --> 00:01:13.251
Are they in the same place?

22
00:01:13.251 --> 00:01:14.601
Pause and guess.

23
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.867
The class says true.

24
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:20.324
Two shelves, one place.

25
00:01:20.324 --> 00:01:23.999
And the log line can't even tell you which shelf it meant.

26
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.613
So the clerk sends you to shelf 12 for a book that lives on shelf 40.

27
00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:30.020
The class isn't lying.

28
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.195
It just never heard about shelves.

29
00:01:32.195 --> 00:01:34.306
So the real problem isn't typing.

30
00:01:34.306 --> 00:01:39.423
Five copies of one list drift apart, the way paperwork does when nobody owns it.

31
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.870
Back to the front desk.

32
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.009
A record is a filled-in form in a clear sleeve.

33
00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:45.878
Let's map it.

34
00:01:45.878 --> 00:01:50.553
Each box on the form is a component: one value that makes up the data.

35
00:01:50.553 --> 00:01:53.826
The clear sleeve means anyone can read every box.

36
00:01:53.826 --> 00:01:56.699
Nothing on the back means no hidden fields.

37
00:01:56.699 --> 00:01:59.571
Two forms are equal when every box matches.

38
00:01:59.571 --> 00:02:03.512
And reading a form aloud means reading every box, in order.

39
00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:06.779
So, wouldn't it be nice to write the list once?

40
00:02:06.779 --> 00:02:08.399
In code, that's one line.

41
00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:15.915
A record is a class whose header lists its components, and javac, the Java compiler, writes the rest from that list.

42
00:02:15.915 --> 00:02:18.053
Kotlin's version is a data class.

43
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:26.224
From that one line, javac writes a private final field for each box, and an accessor that reads it, with no get in front.

44
00:02:26.224 --> 00:02:31.484
One constructor that takes every box in order, called the canonical constructor.

45
00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:35.561
And equals, hashCode and toString, each using every component.

46
00:02:35.561 --> 00:02:37.600
One list, so nothing can drift.

47
00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:39.062
The demo agrees.

48
00:02:39.062 --> 00:02:42.747
toString reads every box: section FIC, shelf 12.

49
00:02:42.747 --> 00:02:45.127
Two equal shelfmarks are equal.

50
00:02:45.127 --> 00:02:47.353
But double equals says false.

51
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:48.839
That's on purpose.

52
00:02:48.839 --> 00:02:53.080
Two forms with the same boxes are still two sheets of paper.

53
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.635
And the form is fixed.

54
00:02:54.635 --> 00:03:00.430
Add a private field in the body, and javac says: field declaration must be static.

55
00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:01.915
No notes on the back.

56
00:03:02.833 --> 00:03:06.070
A form is only as good as the counter that checks it.

57
00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.368
Say the checks live in a factory method, Shelfmark.of.

58
00:03:09.368 --> 00:03:14.864
Then new Shelfmark, with shelf minus three, walks straight past it, through the side door.

59
00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:17.736
A record has a counter with no side door.

60
00:03:17.736 --> 00:03:23.206
Other constructors, factories, copies: every path ends in the canonical constructor.

61
00:03:23.206 --> 00:03:25.355
So that's where the clerk stands.

62
00:03:25.355 --> 00:03:29.067
You write it in the compact form, with no parameter list.

63
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.515
First it rejects a missing section.

64
00:03:32.515 --> 00:03:34.311
Then it checks the shelf.

65
00:03:34.311 --> 00:03:38.981
Last, it tidies the writing, so fic in small letters becomes FIC.

66
00:03:38.981 --> 00:03:42.357
Then javac copies those values into the fields.

67
00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:44.547
Kotlin's twin is an init block.

68
00:03:44.547 --> 00:03:47.359
It can check, but it can't tidy a parameter.

69
00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:51.154
The demo sends in fic 12 and gets FIC 12 back.

70
00:03:51.154 --> 00:03:55.053
Shelf zero is refused: shelf must be 1 or more: 0.

71
00:03:55.053 --> 00:03:58.951
A check that some paths skip is only a suggestion.

72
00:03:58.951 --> 00:04:01.759
Put it in the canonical constructor.

73
00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:04.248
So every form is checked.

74
00:04:04.248 --> 00:04:05.196
Is it safe now?

75
00:04:05.196 --> 00:04:07.220
Here's where the picture breaks.

76
00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.900
A crate record holds a branch, North, and a list of titles, Dune and Emma.

77
00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:17.655
The program keeps the crates it sent in a HashSet, which files each one under its hashCode.

78
00:04:17.867 --> 00:04:21.171
Then someone reuses their list and adds Ulysses.

79
00:04:21.171 --> 00:04:23.718
Does the set still contain the crate?

80
00:04:23.718 --> 00:04:24.820
Pause and guess.

81
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.207
False.

82
00:04:28.207 --> 00:04:30.108
The crate is still in there.

83
00:04:30.108 --> 00:04:34.386
But its hashCode changed, so the set looks in the wrong drawer.

84
00:04:34.386 --> 00:04:35.336
Nothing threw.

85
00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:38.039
The sleeve covers the form's own boxes.

86
00:04:38.039 --> 00:04:43.934
But the list is a sheet stapled behind it, and whoever kept that sheet can still write on it.

87
00:04:43.934 --> 00:04:46.153
The fix is one line at the counter.

88
00:04:46.153 --> 00:04:50.527
List.copyOf gives the record its own copy, one that can't be changed.

89
00:04:51.433 --> 00:04:56.302
So a record is only as frozen as its parts: copy collections on the way in.

90
00:04:56.302 --> 00:05:00.002
And if a class needs a hidden cache, a record can't help.

91
00:05:00.002 --> 00:05:03.507
That one goes back to writing the five places by hand.

92
00:05:04.433 --> 00:05:06.565
So, is a record just a shortcut?

93
00:05:06.565 --> 00:05:06.765
No.

94
00:05:06.765 --> 00:05:10.497
It writes the list once, so the five places can't drift.

95
00:05:10.497 --> 00:05:13.695
It gives you one counter that every form passes.

96
00:05:13.695 --> 00:05:16.093
And its promise stops at the staple.

97
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.110
Quick check.

98
00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.608
A record Masked holds a phone number.

99
00:05:19.608 --> 00:05:23.727
Its accessor is overridden to show only the last four digits.

100
00:05:23.727 --> 00:05:25.415
What does toString print?

101
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:29.681
The full number.

102
00:05:29.681 --> 00:05:33.598
The generated toString reads the field, not your accessor.

103
00:05:33.598 --> 00:05:36.300
To hide a box, override toString itself.
